======================================================================
🚀 DAY 3 NOTES: CLASSES, OBJECTS, DATACLASSES, & MODULES
======================================================================

1. ADVANCED CLASSES & OBJECTS MECHANICS
----------------------------------------------------------------------
* The 'self' Keyword: Represents the specific, individual object 
  currently being handled. It keeps data isolated so objects don't 
  overwrite each other.
* Instance Attributes: Unique data bound to one specific object. 
  Defined inside '__init__' using 'self.variable_name'.
* Class Attributes: Shared global data bound to the blueprint itself. 
  Defined directly under the class name. Every object created shares 
  this exact variable.
* AI Use-Case: Class attributes store the universal framework version 
  (e.g., PyTorch 2.5), while instance attributes store the unique weights 
  of individual AI models.

2. DATACLASSES (THE AI CONFIGURATION SHORTCUT)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Purpose: AI engineering involves writing classes that only store data 
  (hyperparameters, training logs, dataset metadata). Traditional OOP 
  requires writing boring '__init__' methods for every variable.
* The Shortcut: Importing '@dataclass' automates everything. It automatically 
  generates the constructor, type definitions, and a clean print format.
* When to use: Use dataclasses when a class is just a container for data 
  and doesn't require heavy internal logic.

3. MODULES (CLEAN ARCHITECTURE)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Definition: A module is simply a '.py' code file. 
* Purpose: Keeps codebases organized. Instead of one massive 2,000-line 
  file, code is split into single-responsibility files.
* AI Layout: Professional AI architectures split work into separate modules:
  - config.py (settings)
  - data_loader.py (generators)
  - model.py (neural network class blueprints)
  - main.py (execution script)
* Import Rules: Bring modules into other files using:
  'import module_name' OR 'from module_name import specific_class'

======================================================================
📌 KEY SUMMARY:
- Class attributes = Shared globally.
- Instance attributes = Isolated unique data.
- @dataclass = Auto-generates boilerplate constructor code.
- Modules = Structural python files enabling clean imports.
